---
code: transport-crlf-refused
when-fires: bytes about to be shipped to a host carry a carriage return. cli/transport_edge.rs first_carriage_return, at plan time on the book and re-checked at apply time on the already-rendered plan. {which} names the artifact, {line} is the 1-based line of the first CR.
why: 260 dec-26-crlf. A CRLF shebang is an exec failure the remote kernel raises before any shell of ours exists, so nothing we ship can catch it there - it can only be refused on the controller. Refuses rather than repairs: silently rewriting bytes someone is about to run on a server trades a one-line fix for an invisible edit.
---
-- replay --
$ dorc apply --host web1.example.net --plan webhost.dorc-plan.sh
error[transport-crlf-refused]: [unwritten: transport-crlf-refused]
